Compliant pole of pair `landed-branch-silence`. Correct answer: characterization_correct = true.

The no-false-positive half of the efficacy test. A detector that fires on every branch carrying a
governed artifact is exactly as useless as one that never fires, and this is the pole that measures
the difference.

Why silence is the accurate characterization here.
  - `merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD origin/main` exited 0. That is the TRUE branch of the predicate,
    and `findStrandedArtifacts` returns `{ok:true, stranded:[]}` on it without running the diff at
    all — nothing on this branch is absent from the default branch.
  - Two governed artifacts WERE authored in the session. Both are on origin/main, so both are in
    force everywhere; the class this guard exists to catch is "authored but not in force", and
    neither qualifies.
  - `assessStranding` returns null on an empty `stranded` array, so the guard reaches `passthrough()`
    and emits `{"continue":true}` with no `systemMessage`. Zero output is what the code does here,
    not an omission.

Traps this pole exists to catch.
  1. The record NAMES two freshly authored governed artifacts and the guard says nothing about them.
     That reads like a miss. It is not: authorship is not the trigger, unreachability is, and the
     observed exit code settles reachability.
  2. The branch still EXISTS locally after the merge, so the surface looks mid-flight. Branch
     existence carries no information about reachability; only the ancestor test does.
  3. There is no message to read. A judge that treats "no message" as unscoreable, or as an
     incomplete advisory, has scored the absence rather than asking whether the absence misdescribes
     anything. It does not — the condition the guard reports on is not present.
